Los channels son fundamentales para la concurrencia en Go. Aquí hay patrones avanzados:

1. Fan-out, Fan-in: Distribuir trabajo a múltiples workers y combinar resultados.

2. Pipeline: Encadenar stages de procesamiento:

func generator() <-chan int {
    ch := make(chan int)
    go func() {
        for i := 0; i < 10; i++ {
            ch <- i
        }
        close(ch)
    }()
    return ch
}

3. Select con timeout:

select {
case msg := <-ch:
    fmt.Println(msg)
case <-time.After(1 * time.Second):
    fmt.Println("timeout")
}

4. Buffered vs unbuffered: Buffered channels permiten enviar sin receptor inmediato.

Recuerda: quien envía debe cerrar el channel. Nunca cierres desde el receptor.

content_en:
Channels are fundamental for concurrency in Go. Here are advanced patterns:

1. Fan-out, Fan-in: Distribute work to multiple workers and combine results.

2. Pipeline: Chain processing stages:

func generator() <-chan int {
    ch := make(chan int)
    go func() {
        for i := 0; i < 10; i++ {
            ch <- i
        }
        close(ch)
    }()
    return ch
}

3. Select with timeout:

select {
case msg := <-ch:
    fmt.Println(msg)
case <-time.After(1 * time.Second):
    fmt.Println("timeout")
}

4. Buffered vs unbuffered: Buffered channels allow sending without immediate receiver.

Remember: the sender should close the channel. Never close from the receiver.

